// Hong-Ou-Mandel dip - the canonical two-photon interference experiment.
//
// Two indistinguishable photons enter a balanced (50/50) beam splitter, one in
// each input mode. They NEVER exit separately: the two "one photon transmitted,
// one reflected" paths are indistinguishable and interfere destructively, so the
// coincidence probability is exactly zero and the photons always bunch into the
// same output mode.
//
// Run it:
//   omega-run examples/circuits/hom_dip.opticqasm --backend photonics --input 1,1
//
// Expected output (exact, not approximate):
//   |2,0>  0.5      both photons in mode 0
//   |0,2>  0.5      both photons in mode 1
//   |1,1>  0.0      <- the dip: coincidences vanish
//
// Why theta = pi/4 is "balanced": the Rx-convention beam splitter is
//   [[ cos(theta), -e^{i*phi} sin(theta)],
//    [ e^{-i*phi} sin(theta),  cos(theta)]]
// (omega-backend-photonics/src/components.rs). The |1,1> output amplitude is the
// permanent of that 2x2 matrix, cos^2(theta) - sin^2(theta) = cos(2*theta),
// which is zero exactly at theta = pi/4. Splitting ratio is |cos theta|^2 :
// |sin theta|^2 = 50:50 there, hence "balanced".
//
// This is a self-verifying example: the dip is an exact zero, so any nonzero
// |1,1> weight is a bug in the permanent/SLOS path, not numerical noise.

OPTICQASM 1.0;
photon q[2];

// Balanced beam splitter: theta = pi/4 = 0.7853981633974483, phi = 0.
bs_rx(0.7853981633974483, 0.0) q[0], q[1];
